 # Triangle / Box Overlap Test (Conservative 26/6-separating)
 
-Also called: 26-separating and 6-separating Triangle-Box Test, Conservative Triangle-AABB Overlap
+Also called: 26-separating and 6-separating Triangle-Box Test, Conservative Triangle-AABB Overlap, Conservative Triangle/Voxel Overlap Test
 
 ## Overview
-This method determines if an axis-aligned box overlaps a triangle, serving as the core primitive test for conservative voxelization [S1]. It extends the 2D overlap test by Möller and Aila [2005] and Schwarz and Seidel [2010] to 3D by decomposing the problem into a plane-box intersection check and 2D projection tests along the three principal axes [S1]. It is distinguished from its parent [Triangle-AABB Intersection (Separating Axis Theorem)](2050-triangle-aabb-intersection-separating-axis-theorem.md) by optimizing for the specific case of axis-aligned boxes and triangle meshes, using precomputed edge functions to amortize the cost of testing multiple voxels (scanlines) against a single triangle [S1].
+This method determines if a triangle overlaps an axis-aligned box (voxel) for conservative surface voxelization, ensuring that any voxel partially or merely touched by a triangle is marked as occupied [S1, S2]. It differs from standard rasterization, which only sets voxels if the triangle overlaps the voxel center, by using linear edge functions evaluated at critical points to guarantee gap-free coverage [S1]. Unlike the Separating Axis Theorem (SAT) approach [2050-triangle-aabb-intersection-separating-axis-theorem.md], which projects onto 15 axes, this test reduces the problem to a plane overlap check and three 2D projection checks, requiring fewer instructions and registers on GPU hardware [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-The test determines overlap by verifying two conditions: (1) the triangle's plane intersects the box, and (2) the 2D projection of the triangle overlaps the 2D projection of the box along all three axes (for 26-separating) or the dominant axis (for 6-separating) [S1].
+The test decomposes the 3D overlap problem into a plane intersection check and three 2D box-triangle overlap checks (one for each coordinate plane: xy, xz, yz) [S1]. It separates computation into a **Setup Phase** (dependent only on the triangle and voxel size) and an **Actual Test Phase** (dependent on the specific voxel position) [S1, S2].
 
 ### Inputs and Outputs
-*   **Inputs**:
-    *   Triangle vertices $\mathbf{v}_0, \mathbf{v}_1, \mathbf{v}_2$ [S1].
-    *   Triangle normal $\mathbf{n}$ [S1].
-    *   Voxel grid origin $\mathbf{p}$ and voxel diagonal $\Delta \mathbf{p}$ [S1].
-*   **Outputs**: Boolean indicating overlap [S1].
+*   **Inputs:**
+    *   Triangle vertices $\mathbf{v}_0, \mathbf{v}_1, \mathbf{v}_2 \in \mathbb{R}^3$.
+    *   Box minimum corner $\mathbf{p} \in \mathbb{R}^3$.
+    *   Box extent $\Delta \mathbf{p} = (\Delta p_x, \Delta p_y, \Delta p_z) \in \mathbb{R}^3_{>0}$.
+    *   (Optional for 6-separating) Dominant axis of the triangle normal.
+*   **Output:** Boolean `true` if the triangle overlaps the box, `false` otherwise.
 
 ### Data Structures
-The method precomputes 9 floating-point values per triangle to amortize the cost of testing multiple voxels [S1]:
-*   $n_i^x, n_i^y$ for $i \in \{0, 1, 2\}$: Scaled 2D edge normal components [S1].
-*   $d_i^{xy}$ for $i \in \{0, 1, 2\}$: Edge function constants at the critical point [S1].
-
